Sure thing for an example of TypeScript (TS) usage in a function that reverses the order of elements within an array. Here's how it would look like:

```typescript
function reverseArray(inputArr: number[]): number[]{
    return inputArr.reverse();  // This will change original array and returns new arr with same values in backwards direction  
}    
let test = [1,23,45];     
test=reverseArray(test);       /* call the function here to see changes */       
console.log("Reversed Array is:",  test );    // prints reversed array on console          
```         The `inputArr` parameter in this code represents an input for your method, and it's a number[] type because we are dealing with arrays of numbers here (not string or any other types). This function returns the same TypeScript Array after calling .reverse() which changes original array. You call this reverseArray from anywhere else within Your TS App where you want to use reversed version, e.g., for display purpose only and not affect in-place operation of a method like `inputArr` or when using arrays inside loops etc....
